        "student_prefix_sha256": "e0c547d0c3397b75bfd6508d03b62a36614bc852d72e06717187785ff0ea8fa7",
        "target": "Given the problem, I identified the key quantities and the unknown:\n- Total balloons: 52\n- Air per balloon: 5 gallons\n- Total air needed: 52 balloons * 5 gallons/balloon = 260 gallons\n- First 10 minutes rate: 8 gallons/minute\n- Next 5 minutes rate: 4 gallons/minute (half of 8 gallons/minute)\n- After that rate: 2 gallons/minute\n\nTo solve this, I broke the problem into segments and calculated the air filled in each segment:\n1. For the first 10 minutes, I calculated the air filled as 10 minutes * 8 gallons/minute = 80 gallons.\n2. For the next 5 minutes, I calculated the air filled as 5 minutes * 4 gallons/minute = 20 gallons.\n3. The remaining air to be filled was 260 gallons - 80 gallons - 20 gallons = 160 gallons.\n4. To fill the remaining 160 gallons at 2 gallons/minute, I calculated the time as 160 gallons / 2 gallons/minute = 80 minutes.\n\nAdding these segments together, the total time was 10 minutes + 5 minutes + 80 minutes = 95 minutes.\n\nTo check the result, I verified that the total air filled matched the required 260 gallons:\n- 80 gallons (first 10 minutes) + 20 gallons (next 5 minutes) + 160 gallons (remaining time) = 260 gallons.\n\nThe operations I used are reusable in similar problems involving segmented rates and total quantities. I would reuse the method of breaking the problem into segments and calculating the air filled in each segment, then summing the times.\n\nFINAL: 95",
