        "target": "Given the problem, I identified the key quantities: the number of books Jenny wants to read (3), the total number of words in each book (200, 400, and 300 words), and the reading speed (100 words per hour). The unknown I needed to find was the average number of minutes per day Jenny should spend reading.\n\nFirst, I calculated the total number of words Jenny needs to read:\n\\[ 200 + 400 + 300 = 900 \\text{ words} \\]\n\nNext, I determined the total time in hours required to read these words:\n\\[ \\frac{900 \\text{ words}}{100 \\text{ words per hour}} = 9 \\text{ hours} \\]\n\nTo find the average number of minutes per day, I converted the total hours into minutes:\n\\[ 9 \\text{ hours} \\times 60 \\text{ minutes per hour} = 540 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nThen, I divided the total minutes by the number of days to find the average minutes per day:\n\\[ \\frac{540 \\text{ minutes}}{10 \\text{ days}} = 54 \\text{ minutes per day} \\]\n\nTo check the result, I verified that 54 minutes per day for 10 days equals 540 minutes, which is the total time required to read all the books. This check confirms the solution is correct.\n\nThe operation I would reuse is converting total time in hours to total time in minutes and then dividing by the number of days to find the average time per day. This operation is useful whenever you need to distribute a total time evenly over a given period.\n\nFINAL: 54",
